# husk.rc — commands husk runs at startup, exactly as if you had typed them.
#
# The name is the oldest one in the system: "rc" is runcom, "run commands",
# from CTSS in the early 1960s. Louis Pouzin wrote it, and coined the word
# "shell" for the same idea — a decade before Unix had either.
#
# This file is the BUILD's copy: it lives at etc/husk.rc in the source tree
# and is written onto the FAT partition as /fat/husk.rc on every image build.
# You can edit /fat/husk.rc from inside the OS and it will take effect on the
# next boot — but the next `make` puts this version back, so anything you want
# to keep belongs here in the repo.
#
# Lines starting with # are comments. Blank lines are ignored. Everything else
# is a husk command line with the full shell available: pipelines, redirection,
# background (&), $?.

# ── the logging daemon ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Uncommented, this hands logging off from the in-kernel logd to the ring-3
# one, which appends to a file instead of pumping every byte out the serial
# port. Measured on a DEBUG_SCHEDULER|DEBUG_DETAILED boot: ~60-100% of a core
# in the kernel, ~20% here — the serial port was the expense, not the daemon.
# Left commented so a plain build boots exactly as it always has.
#logd /fat/os64.log &
